    {
      "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4A1TUs",
      "body": "SIM, yes. Active plan, no.\r\n\r\nI am oversimplifying here, but this should still get the point across: The SIM is only necessary to give your modem an identity so that towers can talk to it. Towers *do* talk to your equipment, even if it isn't a subscriber of that mobile operator.",
      "author": {
        "login": "Tunas1337",
        "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/12097904?u=fcfbe8a22926df86fae7b75f2ae04bcb097717ab&v=4",
        "url": "https://github.com/Tunas1337"
      },
      "created_at": "2025-08-02T23:12:53Z",
      "updated_at": "2025-08-02T23:12:54Z",
      "is_answer": false,
      "replies": [
        {
          "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4A1Xd3",
          "body": "I think this is a really straightforward answer and we should add it in the docs.\r\n\r\nWe have had those questions before but got lost in nuance because _technically_ it depends on providers. For example:\r\n\r\n* SIM is definitely required.\r\n* Active plan _may be_ required depending on the region and carrier, but _probably_ not. For example, i have a non-activated sim card here that produces a ton of false positive warnings due to not being activated.\r\n* Sometimes people ask this question for the wrong reason. For example they bought a device with the wrong frequency bands and wonder why it can't connect, thinking a new SIM will help here.",
          "author": {
            "login": "untitaker",
            "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/837573?u=4b106f1ba83b3028677a861efe31c46d703e77ff&v=4",
            "url": "https://github.com/untitaker"
